  "answer_text": "Great question! Making eye contact with someone who has a lazy eye (also called strabismus or amblyopia) can feel awkward, but with a little understanding, it doesn't have to be. Here are some tips:\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f Look at the Eye That's Looking at You\nLook at the eye which is looking at you. The person will notice if you are looking at the other eye, and even if you don't intend to be rude, it will feel to them as though you are staring at their \"wonky\" eye.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83e\udde0 Remember \u2014 Both Eyes Are Actually on You\nWhat throws people off is that it looks like one eye is looking at you and one is looking somewhere else. That can make it feel like they're not really making eye contact. Keep in mind that both their eyes are in fact looking at you \u2014 one of them just has a hard time staying centered.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83d\ude0c Don't Pause or Act Confused\nDon't pause and wonder if their attention is on you \u2014 just carry on with what you're saying. The most confusing situation is when both eyes are looking in different directions, but learn to deal with this without looking baffled.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83d\udd0d Try Softening Your Focus (The \"Blurring\" Technique)\nBlurring your eyes lets you look straight at them and listen better, without fixating on which eye to focus on. This is a handy trick that many people find helpful in these situations.\n\n---\n\n### 5. \ud83d\udcac If It's Someone You Know, It's Okay to Acknowledge It\nIf you can't concentrate because of your friend's situation, acknowledge it instead of avoiding eye contact. Even if they don't look at themselves all the time, they know it's distracting. Saying something like \"Sometimes your eye starts moving and it throws me off\" can lighten a possibly awkward situation. Don't use this approach on a salesperson, flight attendant, or a stranger \u2014 just move on. When you're talking to someone you'll probably never see again, it's not worth making them feel self-conscious.\n\n---\n\n### 6. \ud83c\udf1f Focus on the Broader Face\nIf you really feel uneasy, find another point of their face to focus on. You can also shift your gaze naturally during conversation. Much like anything unusual about a person, you will stop noticing it as you get to know them and other things about that person start to form your dominant image.\n\n---\n\nThe most important takeaway is to **be natural and respectful**. People with a lazy eye are very accustomed to this situation and generally just want to have a normal conversation!",
